1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:03,220
I asked my coding agent for one small thing:

2
00:00:03,220 --> 00:00:05,680
give the top bar a bit more room.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:08,040
It changed two lines of CSS.

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,500
Looks fine, right?

5
00:00:09,560 --> 00:00:12,060
So the agent runs selfie review.

6
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:14,860
That renders every story in our Storybook,

7
00:00:14,860 --> 00:00:17,800
124 of them, in a pinned container,

8
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:19,820
right here on this MacBook Air.

9
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:23,760
Twenty-five seconds, with no cloud and no bill per image.

10
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:27,300
What comes back is one JSON document and an exit code,

11
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,080
so any agent can read it.

12
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:31,160
Exit 1, because that two-line

13
00:00:31,160 --> 00:00:34,600
change touched all 124 stories.

14
00:00:34,620 --> 00:00:35,960
Now it’s my turn.

15
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:39,980
The selfie ui command opens the same review for a person:

16
00:00:40,120 --> 00:00:43,580
124 changed, and nothing decided yet.

17
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:45,060
That’s a lot to look at,

18
00:00:45,060 --> 00:00:48,500
but Selfie works out what kind of change each one is.

19
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:51,300
48 of them only moved or gained spacing,

20
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:53,020
with nothing new in the image.

21
00:00:53,020 --> 00:00:53,940
Here’s one.

22
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:55,960
Yellow is spacing that grew.

23
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:58,780
Press F, and the two images flip in place,

24
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,980
so you can see exactly what moved.

25
00:01:00,980 --> 00:01:04,260
That looks right, so I approve those 48 together.

26
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:06,120
But what about dark mode?

27
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:08,660
I search for dark, and there it is:

28
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:11,320
85,000 changed pixels.

29
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:13,420
The agent hard-coded a light color,

30
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:15,180
and the top bar went white.

31
00:01:15,180 --> 00:01:17,060
I don’t have to describe where.

32
00:01:17,120 --> 00:01:19,280
I click the change, write what’s wrong,

33
00:01:19,280 --> 00:01:21,740
and send it to the agent, which has been waiting.

34
00:01:21,740 --> 00:01:25,320
The agent gets my words, the story, the exact box,

35
00:01:25,320 --> 00:01:27,140
and a picture of what I saw.

36
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:28,960
It puts the theme’s color back,

37
00:01:28,960 --> 00:01:31,360
renders again, and answers my note.

38
00:01:31,380 --> 00:01:33,240
Selfie tells me there’s a newer batch,

39
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:35,680
and the reply is right there on the story.

40
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,440
The bar is dark again, and 85,000

41
00:01:38,440 --> 00:01:40,980
changed pixels are down to 13.

42
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:43,460
I resolve the note, and approve the rest.

43
00:01:43,460 --> 00:01:46,780
The 48 I already approved are simply unchanged now,

44
00:01:46,780 --> 00:01:49,340
because an approval follows your Git history.

45
00:01:49,340 --> 00:01:51,920
The agent commits and reviews once more.

46
00:01:51,920 --> 00:01:54,920
It’s the exact same build, so nothing renders.

47
00:01:54,920 --> 00:01:57,600
A third of a second, and exit 0.

48
00:01:57,600 --> 00:01:59,960
Push it, and Selfie renders in CI

49
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:01,860
and puts a status on the commit.

50
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:05,300
Click through, and it’s the same review, in the cloud.

51
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:07,640
Selfie works with any coding agent.

52
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:08,960
If you have a Storybook,

53
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:11,600
start locally today at selfie.dev,

54
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:13,860
and join the waitlist for the cloud.
